Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a rounding of constant radius whose contact line folds over
itself on a face, where its edge bends towards that face more tightly
than the ball touches it from the edge, is walked across the folds and as
several pieces, the twisted one across each fold between untwisted ones,
so that OCCT's own filling of twisted pieces builds it (mitcad#2228).

The case: a band 24 wide and 10 high between two vertical walls along two
waves (amplitude 8, wavelength 30), its walls bending at the crests with
a radius of 2.77 mm. Its top edge along a wave, rounded with 2.5, builds;
with 3, 5 or 8.5 it fails: "the walk between the two faces stops part of
the way" (one piece computed). A ball of radius r touches the top face
r tan(a / 2) from the edge (r for the right angle here); where the edge
bends towards the top face with a radius under that (each crest), the
contact line, the edge moved that far into the face, runs backwards
between two cusps and crosses itself in a swallowtail. The ball's
sections exist all along. But BRepBlend_Walking::CheckDeflection takes a
step whose chord on a face points against the contact's tangent at the
point before as a step back of the walk (Blend_Backward, then too large):
at a cusp the contact turns back, so every step across it was refused and
the walk stopped there. Past the cusp the walk would be twisted on that
face (BlendFunc_ConstRad::TwistOnS1: the contact's tangent against the
guide). PerformSetOfKGen reprocesses a twisted piece between two
untwisted neighbours on the same faces: it cuts the neighbours where
their contact lines cross (ChFi3d_IntTraces: the corner where the
swallowtail is cut away) and fills the piece between them with a
constrained filling. A rounding along one edge is one walk, one piece,
with no neighbour: "adjustment by reprocessing the non-written points:
no neighbor", ChFiDS_TwistedSurface.

- BRepBlend_Walking::CheckDeflection: where a contact's tangent points
  along the guide at the point before and against it at the new point (or
  the other way), and the contact moved no farther than the other contact
  did, the contact turned back at a cusp of its line between them: the
  step is not judged by that contact (Blend_OK for it), the other
  contact's check stands, only where the other contact keeps moving (its
  speed at both points at least a twentieth of the guide's: where the ball
  itself nearly stands still a contact turning back is the walk going over
  to another branch; on the reported wave with 8.5 the wall contact turned
  so at w 12.80 and the walk ran back to the edge's start, "Chaining is
  impossible"); only a short step (at most 5 % of the walk's
  largest step, at least 2 tolgui) is taken so, a longer one is too large
  and halved: the sections between the walk's points are solved again
  from a cubic through the contacts and their tangents (patch 1870), which
  follows a cusp only over a short step (with 8.5 the steps of 0.38 across
  the cusps left the stripe failing after the walk).
- BlendFunc_ConstRad (and BlendFunc_ConstRadInv)::SetSectionOnContact:
  the guide's normal plane can hold the contact on one surface instead of
  the contacts' mid-point. The mid-point's line, the edge moved half way
  to the contacts, folds as well where the edge bends towards it more
  tightly than that distance: there the mid-point's condition holds two
  balls that meet (the equations singular), and the walk went over to the
  other one (on the reported wave with 8.5, at w 9.8, where the fold ratio
  on the top face passes 2: the wall contact, whose line is the edge
  moved down, turned back there and ran to the edge's start by w 13.09,
  and the stripe failed: "Standard_DomainError"). The contact whose line
  does not fold picks one ball all along; where the guide is the edge and
  the faces meet square, it is the same ball as the mid-point's.
  ChFi3d_FoldCutsOf notes, per spine, each edge whose contact line folds,
  its faces and stretches, and the face whose contact line does not fold
  there (no stretch, the largest fold ratio below 0.9);
  ChFi3d_FilBuilder::PerformSurf and PerformFirstSection walk across such
  a stretch with that face's contact in the plane (ChFi3d_FoldWalkOf).
- BRepBlend_Walking::InternalPerform: once the walk is twisted (has gone
  over a cusp of a contact line), its steps are at most 5 % of its
  largest step (at least 2 tolgui): the contact line folds back near the
  walk there, and a long step's start lies nearer another branch of the
  equations than the walk's own (on the reported wave with 8.5 a step of
  0.168 from w 12.632 landed on a branch whose wall contact lay 0.086
  off the walk's own, which folds at w 12.8015; the walk died there:
  "CallPerformSurf : Path failed!").
- BRepBlend_Walking::TestArret: a step in which a contact moves more than
  fifty times the guide's own advance (and 100 tolpoint3d) is too large
  and halved, also where the step is too short for the deflection's test:
  the walk has gone over to another solution, a ball touching the face
  elsewhere (on the reported wave with 8.5 the wall contact jumped 6.6 mm
  to the crest's other flank in a step of 0.0007 at w 12.80, and the walk
  ran back along that ball to the edge's start: "Chaining is
  impossible").
- BRepBlend_AppFuncRoot::SearchPoint: where the solver does not converge
  from the linear start between two of the walk's points, it starts again
  from each of those points, the nearer first (sections themselves); only
  where neither converges is the section not found. A section (of a blend
  of four variables) that lies farther from the linear start than twice
  the two points lie apart, and at least 0.01, is solved again from each of them, and the
  solution nearest the linear start kept: where the contact line folds
  over itself the ball's centre may lie where a line crosses the contact
  line's other branches (millimetres apart with 8.5), and the solver from
  the linear start landed there; the approximation of the folded piece
  through such sections reached 679 mm, and the body came out invalid.
- ChFi3d_Builder::CompleteData (two faces), for a twisted piece: the
  approximation takes the sections' values alone (GeomAbs_C0), not their
  derivatives along the guide. With 8.5 the ball nearly stands still on
  the crest (the wall contact kept still from w 12.85 to 13.0 while the
  guide ran on), the derivatives through the near-singular Jacobian were
  no measure of the smooth sections, and the approximation ran to 65006
  poles and 679 mm off the walk ("done", as AdvApprox has a result
  whatever its error). What the filling takes of the piece (its contacts'
  curves and ends) the values give within the tolerance.
- ChFi3d_Builder::CompleteData (two faces), for a twisted piece: its
  contacts' curves on the faces are interpolated through the walk's own
  contacts at the walk's parameters (taken before the approximation adds
  its sections to the line), not taken from the approximation: between
  the walk's points the approximation's sections lay on another branch of
  the equations near the ball that nearly stands still (the wall contact
  kept still at (-21.017, 19.617) from w 12.85 to 13.0 where the walk's
  moved from u 12.63 to 12.96), and the filling's side on the wall through
  them looped over itself (34.8 long where the walk's contact runs 7.5 at
  the wall's v = -1.5): the wall's wire crossed itself and the body was
  invalid.
- ChFi3d_Builder::StoreData, for a twisted piece (whose surface
  PerformSetOfKGen's filling replaces): its normals, which orient the
  piece, count as vanishing only below Precision::Confusion(), not below
  the tolerance its approximation reached (an area against a length;
  with 8.5 the folded piece's approximation was done, then StoreData
  found no orientation and the stripe failed: "PerformSurf : Failed
  approximation!").
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfKPart: after its guides are made, for a
  fillet of constant radius along an open spine (not a chamfer with
  penetration), the stretches of each edge where the contact line on one
  of its faces folds (ChFi3d_FoldsOfContact: the edge's curvature towards
  the face's inside times r tan(a / 2) above 1, sampled about every
  twentieth of the radius and halved to the border) split the guides a
  tenth of each stretch's length (at least 10 tolesp) before and after
  it (ChFi3d_FoldCuts, ChFi3d_SplitGuidesAtFolds); stretches whose
  margins meet are one, one reaching within its margin of the spine's
  ends is not split off (it would have no neighbour there).
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine: a guide's end at such a cut
  is not extended by the 1 % that helps rsnld: the walk ends there, where
  the next guide's piece starts (extended, the first piece ran 1 % on
  into the fold, and the next piece, starting from that piece's end past
  its own first parameter, found no path).
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfKGen: a guide that starts at such a cut,
  where the guide before it ends, starts from the last piece walked (its
  Previous), as a guide after a particular piece does; without one its
  walk removed the pieces walked before it (RemoveSD of two null ends).
- With the walk traced (MITCAD_TRACE_FILLET_WALK, port patch 2141): each
  edge's largest fold ratio on each face, each folding stretch, each fold
  not split off, each split and each contact turning back at a cusp,
  each section the approximation does not find or solves again far from
  the walk (SearchPoint), each walked
  line whose approximation is not done (CompleteData), each twisted
  piece's walked sections (the arc between the contacts, the centre, the
  axis and the contacts, jumps marked: CompleteData), the tolerance each twisted
  piece's approximation reached and how far its surface's sides lie from
  the walked contacts (StoreData), each piece StoreData finds no
  orientation for (with the normals and tolerances), and
  (ChFi3d_Builder::Compute) the exception that fails a stripe's
  computation, on the standard error.

Only a stripe that fails without them is computed with these rules, and
in it only the walk across a fold takes the walk's rules:
- ChFi3d_Builder::Compute: every stripe is computed first as before, no
  fold looked for (ChFi3d_FoldCutsOf finds none while the fold stripes'
  mode is off). A stripe that fails, along an edge where the contact line
  on one of its faces folds (ChFi3d_ContactLineFolds), is computed again
  with the mode on (ChFi3d_SetFoldStripes, per thread, restored after
  it): its guides split around the folds, the walk across each fold
  twisted; as it was where that fails too. A stripe that builds is never
  computed so, whatever its contact lines do. (With the rules for every
  walk, or with every edge whose contact line folds split, roundings that
  built before failed: a three faces' corner whose short concave arcs
  bend the edge more tightly than the ball of 3 touches the rod, the
  ball leaving the arc's face before the contact on the rod would turn
  back; an acute concave turn; a pierced face; a ball standing still.)
- In that second pass ChFi3d_FilBuilder::PerformSurf and
  PerformFirstSection mark a walk between an edge's faces whose guide
  runs across a stretch where its contact line folds
  (ChFi3d_FoldWalkOf), for the walk, its approximation and its storing
  (BRepBlend_SetFoldWalk, per thread, restored after it): the cusp step,
  the short twisted steps, the jump guard (BRepBlend_Walking), the
  sections searched again (BRepBlend_AppFuncRoot), the twisted piece of
  values alone, its contacts' curves and its orientation (CompleteData,
  StoreData) and the section on the contact that does not fold apply
  only to such a walk; the walks before and after it are OCCT's.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2228.
